@@ -16,4 +16,27 @@ import { resolvePointer } from "./src/index.ts";
 resolvePointer({ users: [{ name: "ada" }] }, "/users/0/name"); // "ada"
 ```
 
+## JSON Patch
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies a JSON Patch ([RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902)) to a
+JSON document, and returns the resulting document as a new value: neither the document nor the patch is mutated.
+The patch is an array of the six operations `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`, applied in order.
+It throws an error when the patch is invalid, or when one of its operations fails; no partial result is returned.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyPatch({ users: [{ name: "ada" }] }, [
+  { op: "replace", path: "/users/0/name", value: "grace" },
+  { op: "add", path: "/users/-", value: { name: "linus" } },
+]); // { users: [{ name: "grace" }, { name: "linus" }] }
+```
+
+Notes on the RFC's rules:
+
+- `add` into an array inserts at the index, and `-` appends; `add` into an object sets the member.
+- `remove`, `replace`, `move` (from) and `copy` (from) require their source to exist.
+- `move` cannot move a value into one of its own children.
+- `test` compares JSON values: object key order is ignored, array order is not.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test`.
