+---
+id: task-002-json-patch-rfc-6902-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "JSON Patch (RFC 6902) applyPatch"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# JSON Patch (RFC 6902) applyPatch
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches. Implement JSON Patch, as specified in
+RFC 6902, with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`. It builds on
+JSON Pointer (task-001), which it uses to address the locations an operation changes.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`.
+- `patch` is an array of operations. Applying it returns the resulting document as a new value; the input
+  document and the input patch are never mutated.
+- Throw an error when the patch is invalid (not an array, an operation that is not an object, an unknown
+  `op`, a missing or mistyped member, or an invalid pointer).
+- Throw an error when one of the operations fails. The whole patch is then rejected: no partial result is
+  returned.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- `add` on an object member sets it, replacing the member when it already exists.
+- `add` on an array inserts the value at the index; index `-` appends; an index beyond the end is an error.
+- `add` with the empty pointer `""` replaces the whole document.
+- `remove` deletes an object member or splices out an array element; the target must exist; `-` and the
+  empty pointer are errors.
+- `replace` sets an existing target; the target must exist. The empty pointer replaces the whole document.
+- `move` removes the value at `from` and adds it at `path`; `from` must exist, and must not be a proper
+  prefix of `path` (a value cannot move into itself).
+- `copy` adds a copy of the value at `from` to `path`; the copy shares no structure with the source, so
+  later operations on one do not change the other.
+- `test` succeeds when the value at `path` equals `value` under JSON equality (object key order is ignored,
+  array order is not); otherwise it fails.
+- Operations are applied in order, each to the result of the previous one.
+- Object members are set as own properties: a key such as `__proto__` is stored as data, not as a prototype
+  change.
+- Values taken from the patch are copied into the result, so the input patch is not changed by later
+  operations.
+- Tests cover each operation's happy path, and its error paths, including the examples of RFC 6902
+  appendix A, and run with `npm test`.
+
+## Requirements traceability
+
+- Feature: JSON Patch support for the json-toolkit library.
+- Standard: RFC 6902 (JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Patch).
+- Depends on: task-001 (JSON Pointer, RFC 6901).
+- Consumers: the other team projects that import this library (see README.md).
